= 1
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

The relevant comparison is between <gravity> and <surface tension>. With the <mass density> difference $\Delta\rho\simeq10^3\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, the <Bond number> and <capillary length> are
$$
\mathrm{Bo}=\frac{\Delta\rho\,g r^2}{\gamma}
=\frac{10^3(9.81)(5\times10^{-4})^2}{0.072}\simeq0.034,
\qquad
\ell_c=\sqrt{\frac{\gamma}{\Delta\rho\,g}}\simeq2.71\,\mathrm{mm}.
$$
Thus the drop is small compared with the <capillary length>; <hydrostatic pressure> variations only weakly perturb its shape. The <Young–Laplace equation> then gives approximately constant interfacial <mean curvature>, and an axisymmetric drop on a uniform flat substrate is a <spherical cap>. The substrate selects the <contact angle>, measured through the <water>.

Write $R$ for the radius of the generating <sphere>, $s$ for the contact-circle radius, and $h$ for the apex height. Elementary <trigonometric identities> give
$$
s=R\sin\theta,\qquad h=R(1-\cos\theta)=s\tan(\theta/2).
$$
Taking the stated droplet radius to mean its radius of <curvature>, $R=r$, gives
$$
\boxed{h=0.5(1-\cos55^\circ)\,\mathrm{mm}=0.213\,\mathrm{mm}.}
$$
There is no diagram or explicit radius convention in the original PDF. If $r$ instead denotes the contact-circle radius, the answer is $h=r\tan27.5^\circ=0.260\,\mathrm{mm}$. If it denotes the radius of an equal-volume free <sphere>, use the <volume of a spherical cap>, $\pi h^2(3R-h)/3=4\pi r^3/3$, to obtain $h=r[4(1-\cos\theta)/(2+\cos\theta)]^{1/3}=0.436\,\mathrm{mm}$. The <spherical cap> approximation is justified in all three conventions; a unique numerical height requires specifying which radius is meant.

\Image[2022-q1-cap.png]
{title=Geometry of the <spherical cap> and its <contact angle>}
{description=Original cross-section distinguishing the generating-sphere radius $R$, contact-circle radius $s$, and height $h$. The dashed part of the generating circle lies below the substrate.}

= 2
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

By definition, the <relaxation modulus> is the <stress> generated by a unit step of <strain> from an initially relaxed state. Set $\epsilon(t)=H(t)$, where $H$ is the <Heaviside step function>, and write $\sigma(t)=G(t)$. Integrating the <linear ordinary differential equation> across the step shows that neither $G$ nor $G'$ can jump: a jump would produce a <Dirac delta function> or its <derivative>, absent from the forcing. Hence $G(0^+)=G'(0^+)=0$.

For $t>0$, a particular solution is $1$. The <characteristic equation> factors as
$$
15\lambda^2+8\lambda+1=(5\lambda+1)(3\lambda+1)=0,
$$
so $G=1+A e^{-t/5}+B e^{-t/3}$. The initial conditions give $A+B=-1$ and $A/5+B/3=0$, whence
$$
\boxed{G(t)=\left(1-\frac52e^{-t/5}+\frac32e^{-t/3}\right)H(t).}
$$
The time and modulus units are those implicit in the printed coefficients. A <Laplace transform> check gives $\widehat G(s)=1/[s(5s+1)(3s+1)]$.

The original PDF really does put undifferentiated <strain> on the right-hand side. The resulting response rises from zero to one, with $G'(t)=\tfrac12(e^{-t/5}-e^{-t/3})>0$, rather than exhibiting ordinary <stress relaxation>. There is a stronger physical problem if this is intended as a passive material law: with the convention $e^{i\omega t}$ the <stress>-to-<strain> amplitude ratio is
$$
G^*(\omega)=\frac1{1-15\omega^2+8i\omega},\qquad
G''(\omega)=\frac{-8\omega}{(1-15\omega^2)^2+64\omega^2}<0\quad(\omega>0).
$$
The <loss modulus> would give negative cycle-averaged supplied power $\langle\sigma\dot\epsilon\rangle=\omega G''|\epsilon_0|^2/2$, contrary to <passivity in linear viscoelasticity>. The boxed expression is therefore the formal <relaxation modulus> of the equation as printed, not a claim that this equation describes an ordinary passive <viscoelasticity> model. No derivative of the input has been silently inserted.

= 3
{parent=Section A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Treat the <liposome> using the usual incompressible-tail packing model of a <lipid bilayer>: neglect the head-group thickness and let $v$ be the volume filling the hydrophobic part of the <lipid vesicle>. Both <membrane leaflets> have area $a$ per head group. Put $d=v/a$, and let $R$ be the unknown outer radius. Counting <molecules> from the inner and outer <surface areas> gives
$$
M_i=\frac{4\pi r_i^2}{a},\qquad M_o=\frac{4\pi R^2}{a}.
$$
Equating the total molecular volume to the volume of the <spherical shell> therefore gives
$$
\frac{4\pi}{3}(R^3-r_i^3)=v(M_i+M_o),\qquad
\boxed{R^3-r_i^3=3d(R^2+r_i^2).}
$$
To prove uniqueness in the physical domain $R>r_i>0$, define
$$
q(R)=\frac{R^3-r_i^3}{R^2+r_i^2}.
$$
Its <derivative> is
$$
q'(R)=\frac{R^4+3R^2r_i^2+2Rr_i^3}{(R^2+r_i^2)^2}>0.
$$
Also $q(r_i)=0$ and $q(R)\to\infty$ as $R\to\infty$. By <continuity> and strict <monotonicity>, $q(R)=3d$ has **exactly one solution with $R>r_i$**. This proves the required uniqueness without choosing among ambiguous branches of a cubic formula.

The maximum tail length is a physical admissibility bound, not automatically the actual leaflet thickness. If $r_m$ is the radius where the two tail regions meet, separate <membrane leaflet> volume balances give
$$
r_m^3=r_i^3+3d r_i^2,\qquad
R^3-r_m^3=3d R^2.
$$
The unique candidate must satisfy
$$
\boxed{r_m-r_i\leq l,\qquad R-r_m\leq l.}
$$
In particular $R-r_i\leq2l$ is necessary. The premise that a <lipid vesicle> has assembled supplies this compatibility: arbitrary positive choices of $r_i,v,a,l$ need not allow any such <lipid bilayer>. For a very large vesicle the two thicknesses tend to $d$, so $R-r_i\simeq2v/a$. Setting the thickness equal to $2l$ for every <molecule> would confuse maximum extension with actual packing. The volume-balance argument and its domain are an example of <uniqueness of the packed spherical bilayer radius>.

= Section B
