= 4
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For an <ideal chain>, independent <Kuhn length> steps give the <random walk> relation
$$
\langle R_{\mathrm{sub}}^2\rangle=g b^2,\qquad \boxed{\sigma\sim b\sqrt g,\quad g\sim(\sigma/b)^2,}
$$
where $R_{\mathrm{sub}}$ denotes the end-to-end displacement of a subchain. Equivalently the mass-size <fractal> exponent is $2$. Numerical factors depend on the chosen definition of blob diameter.

A subchain smaller than the adsorption height explores three dimensions much as a free <ideal chain> does. At scales exceeding $\xi$, <adsorption> restricts its perpendicular extent to order $\xi$, so the coil is anisotropic. Its in-plane projection may still have a <random walk> law for an <ideal chain>, but the isotropic three-dimensional blob construction cannot be extrapolated to $\sigma>\xi$. This is the reason to use <adsorption blobs> of size $\xi$.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Here $T$ is the absolute <temperature> and $k_B$ the <Boltzmann constant>. An <adsorption blob> contains $g\sim(\xi/b)^2$ <monomers>, so there are $N/g\sim N(b/\xi)^2$ such blobs. Confining a blob removes an amount of conformational <entropy> of order $k_B$; thus the positive confinement contribution to the <Helmholtz free energy> is
$$
F_{\mathrm{conf}}\simeq k_BT\frac Ng=k_BT N\left(\frac b\xi\right)^2.
$$
The statement about one blob's <entropy> is used as an <entropy> *loss* relative to the free chain, rather than as an absolute <entropy>.

In the assumed slab model, contacts occur within a monomer-scale distance $b$ of the substrate. The contact fraction is approximately $b/\xi$, giving $N_c\simeq Nb/\xi$. Multiplying this by the negative contact <energy> yields
$$
F_{\mathrm{contact}}=-\epsilon k_BT\frac{Nb}{\xi},\qquad
\boxed{F(\xi)=Nk_BT\left[\left(\frac b\xi\right)^2-\epsilon\frac b\xi\right].}
$$
The two contributions express the competition between loss of conformational <entropy> and gain of <adsorption> <energy>. The numerical coefficients are the ones stipulated by this blob approximation.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Minimize the <Helmholtz free energy> at fixed $N,T$. Its <derivative> is
$$
\frac{\partial F}{\partial\xi}=Nk_BT\left(-\frac{2b^2}{\xi^3}+\frac{\epsilon b}{\xi^2}\right).
$$
The physical <stationary point> for $\epsilon>0$ is
$$
\boxed{\xi_* =\frac{2b}{\epsilon},\qquad F_{\mathrm{ads},*}=-\frac14Nk_BT\epsilon^2.}
$$
To check the minimum directly, put $x=b/\xi$. Then $F/(Nk_BT)=(x-\epsilon/2)^2-\epsilon^2/4$, which is globally minimized at $x=\epsilon/2$. The binding <free energy> gain per <monomer> is consequently $\mu=k_BT\epsilon^2/4>0$.

The weak-<adsorption> blob picture requires $b\ll\xi_*\ll b\sqrt N$, or $2/\sqrt N\ll\epsilon\ll2$ in this model. If $\epsilon$ is too large, the layer saturates at a monomer-scale thickness; if too small, a many-blob adsorbed layer has not formed.

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The stretched segment is a <Gaussian chain> of $n$ <Kuhn length> steps, with unperturbed root-mean-square end-to-end distance $l=b\sqrt n$. Its end-to-end <probability density> is proportional to $\exp[-3z^2/(2nb^2)]$, so its leading elastic <Helmholtz free energy> is $3k_BTz^2/(2nb^2)$. Adding the remaining segment's <adsorption> <free energy> gives
$$
\boxed{F(n,z)=k_BT(N-n)\left[\left(\frac b\xi\right)^2-\epsilon\frac b\xi\right]
+\frac{3k_BTz^2}{2nb^2}.}
$$
An additive constant independent of $n,z$ is irrelevant. At the optimal adsorption height this becomes
$$
F(n,z)=-(N-n)\mu+\frac{3k_BTz^2}{2nb^2},\qquad \mu=\frac14k_BT\epsilon^2.
$$
The original PDF's hint labels the positive quantity proportional to $k_BT R^2/l^2$ an <entropy>. It has units of <energy> and is the stretching <free energy>. The corresponding change of conformational <entropy> is $\Delta S=-3k_B R^2/(2l^2)$. This dimensional and sign correction is essential to the minimization.

This is the leading extensive <Gaussian chain> approximation: endpoint normalization and junction corrections give subleading terms. It requires many stretched <monomers> and $z\ll nb$, rather than extension near the full contour length.

= e
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

During slow peeling in <atomic force microscopy>, $n$ adjusts to minimize the <Helmholtz free energy> at each imposed height. Define the positive binding gain $\mu=k_BT[\epsilon b/\xi-(b/\xi)^2]$ for the adsorbed segment. The <stationary point> satisfies
$$
0=\left(\frac{\partial F}{\partial n}\right)_z
=\mu-\frac{3k_BTz^2}{2b^2n^2},\qquad
n_*(z)=\frac zb\sqrt{\frac{3k_BT}{2\mu}}.
$$
Since $\partial_n^2F=3k_BTz^2/(b^2n^3)>0$, this is a minimum while $0<n_*<N$. The upward pulling <force> is the height <derivative> of the minimized <free energy>. The implicit $n_*(z)$ term vanishes because $\partial_nF=0$, giving
$$
f=\frac{dF(n_*(z),z)}{dz}
=\frac{3k_BTz}{b^2n_*}
=\frac{\sqrt{6k_BT\mu}}b.
$$
Using $\xi=2b/\epsilon$,
$$
\boxed{f_{\mathrm{peel}}=\sqrt{\frac32}\,\frac{\epsilon k_BT}{b}.}
$$
It is independent of $z$: extra pulling height releases more <monomers> while leaving the fractional extension of the stretched part unchanged. Equivalently $F_{\min}=-N\mu+f_{\mathrm{peel}}z$. This is the <equilibrium peeling force of an adsorbed Gaussian chain>.

The plateau ends when $n_*=N$, at
$$
z_{\mathrm{det}}=Nb\sqrt{\frac{2\mu}{3k_BT}}=\frac{Nb\epsilon}{\sqrt6}.
$$
Thereafter all <monomers> are detached and the <Gaussian chain> force is $f=3k_BTz/(Nb^2)$, agreeing continuously with the plateau at $z_{\mathrm{det}}$. Very near the start and final release, finite-chain and junction effects lie outside the blob approximation.

= f
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

For the usual interpretation of a real chain in a <good solvent>, the detachment <force> is **smaller** in the weak-<adsorption> regime. <Excluded volume> swells the chain, increasing the cost of confinement near the substrate. The adsorbed layer becomes thicker and its binding <free energy> per <monomer> decreases. The same contact <energy> therefore does not mean the same total binding strength.

The scaling argument can include both adsorption and stretching. Let $\xi\sim bg^\nu$, where $\nu=1/2$ for an <ideal chain> and approximately $3/5$ for a <self-avoiding walk> in three dimensions. With the same contact-fraction approximation,
$$
\frac{F_{\mathrm{ads}}}{Nk_BT}\sim
\left(\frac b\xi\right)^{1/\nu}-\epsilon\frac b\xi.
$$
Minimization gives $\xi/b\sim\epsilon^{-\nu/(1-\nu)}$ and $\mu/(k_BT)\sim\epsilon^{1/(1-\nu)}$. At detachment, a <tensile blob> of size $\xi_t\sim k_BT/f$ costs approximately $k_BT$; its <monomer> count is $g_t\sim(\xi_t/b)^{1/\nu}$. Equating the stretching gain per <monomer> $k_BT/g_t$ to $\mu$ gives
$$
f\sim\frac{k_BT}{b}\left(\frac{\mu}{k_BT}\right)^\nu
\sim\frac{k_BT}{b}\epsilon^{\nu/(1-\nu)}.
$$
Thus $f_{\mathrm{real}}\sim(k_BT/b)\epsilon^{3/2}$, compared with $f_{\mathrm{ideal}}\sim(k_BT/b)\epsilon$, so their ratio scales as $\epsilon^{1/2}<1$ for weak attraction. These are scaling relations, not exact prefactors. The real-chain adsorption exponents here use the same simple uniform-contact model as the earlier parts; a more detailed surface theory of <critical phenomena> can refine them. The thicker, more weakly bound layer reflects the higher cost of confining a <good solvent> chain.https://dasher.wustl.edu/bio5357/readings/rubinstein-chapter3.pdf[Rubinstein and Colby, Polymer Physics, chapter 3, equations (3.59)–(3.66)]{ref}

“Real” alone does not specify <solvent> quality: a collapsed chain in a poor <solvent>, strong adsorption, or substantial bending stiffness requires a different comparison. The smaller-force conclusion concerns the flexible <good solvent> chain implicit in the excluded-volume correction.

= 5
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The gap is not a fixed mechanical separation. <Thermal fluctuations> of the wall compete with drift towards the <microtubule> under the opposing <force>. A <monomer> can be incorporated only after the gap reaches its length. The <Smoluchowski equation> describes this overdamped drift and <diffusion> of gap <probability density>.

For gap potential <energy> $U(x)=Fx$, the equation and its <probability current> are
$$
\partial_t p=-\partial_xJ,
\qquad J=-D\left(\partial_xp+\beta U'(x)p\right)
=-D(p_x+\beta Fp),\qquad \beta=(k_BT)^{-1}.
$$
The drift mobility is $D\beta$, the overdamped consequence of the <fluctuation-dissipation relation for a Langevin particle>. Equivalently, this coefficient is fixed by requiring the zero-current solution to be the <Boltzmann distribution> $p\propto e^{-\beta U}$. Solve this equation with the appropriate exclusion, incorporation and probability-normalization conditions. The steady flux through the incorporation threshold gives the addition rate $j_0$; the elongation speed is $v=\delta j_0$.

There is a regime distinction in the printed problem. The absorbing boundary imposed in part (c) means instantaneous incorporation once a gap opens, and leads to the <diffusion-limited Brownian ratchet with constant load>. A genuinely <reaction-limited polymerization Brownian ratchet> instead assumes fast gap equilibration followed by a finite chemical addition rate, giving an exponential gating factor multiplying that chemical rate. The equations requested in the subsequent parts specify the former model, which is the one solved here.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

In the <steady state>, conservation of <probability> requires $J_x=0$, so $J=j_0$ is constant. The sign is positive because increasing gap reaches the incorporation boundary, whereas the deterministic drift points towards smaller gap. Thus
$$
p'(x)+\beta Fp(x)=-\frac{j_0}{D}.
$$
An <integrating factor> $e^{\beta Fx}$ gives
$$
\boxed{p(x)=A e^{-\beta Fx}-\frac{j_0}{D\beta F}\quad(F>0).}
$$
Substitution makes the check particularly transparent:
$$
p'=-\beta F A e^{-\beta Fx},\qquad
-D(p'+\beta Fp)=j_0,
$$
so $\partial_t p=-J_x=0$. The constant offset is necessary for nonzero transport; a pure <Boltzmann distribution> would have $J=0$ and could not describe a steady succession of addition events. The $F=0$ limit is taken after applying the boundary conditions, avoiding the apparent singularity of the displayed parametrization.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

In this <polymerization Brownian ratchet> model, a gap of width $\delta$ immediately accommodates one <monomer>. That event advances the tip by $\delta$ and resets the gap to zero. The threshold is therefore an <absorbing boundary condition for a diffusion>,
$$
\boxed{p(\delta)=0.}
$$
All waiting-time <probability> lies within $0\leq x<\delta$, so the steady occupation <probability density> satisfies
$$
\boxed{\int_0^\delta p(x)\,dx=1.}
$$
There is a reinjection flux $j_0$ at $x=0$ equal to the absorption flux at $x=\delta$. The nonpenetration constraint at zero is a <reflecting boundary condition for a diffusion> during each individual waiting episode. For the stationary reset process it must not be confused with setting the net current $J(0^+)$ to zero: the source at reset supplies $J(0^+)=j_0$. This is how a normalized steady density can coexist with a nonzero exit flux.

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Put $k=\beta F$ and $q=k\delta$. The <absorbing boundary condition for a diffusion> gives $A=j_0e^q/(Dk)$, hence
$$
p(x)=\frac{j_0}{Dk}\left[e^{k(\delta-x)}-1\right].
$$
This is nonnegative throughout the allowed interval. Normalizing the <probability density> gives
$$
1=\frac{j_0}{Dk}\left[\frac{e^q-1}{k}-\delta\right]
=\frac{j_0}{Dk^2}(e^q-1-q).
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{j_0=\frac{D\beta^2F^2}{e^{\beta F\delta}-\beta F\delta-1},\qquad
A=\frac{\beta F\,e^{\beta F\delta}}{e^{\beta F\delta}-\beta F\delta-1}.}
$$
Equivalently the normalized density is
$$
p(x)=\frac{k[e^{k(\delta-x)}-1]}{e^q-1-q}.
$$
The denominator is positive for $q>0$, since $e^q>1+q$. The dimensions are consistent: $j_0$ has units of inverse time, $A$ and $p$ of inverse length, and $q$ is dimensionless.

= e
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For a small opposing <force>, the relevant parameter is $q=\beta F\delta\ll1$. A <Taylor series> gives
$$
e^q-1-q=\frac{q^2}{2}\left(1+\frac q3+\frac{q^2}{12}+\cdots\right),
$$
so
$$
\boxed{j_0=\frac{2D}{\delta^2}\left(1-\frac q3+\frac{q^2}{36}+O(q^3)\right)
\longrightarrow\frac{2D}{\delta^2}.}
$$
In particular the zero-force rate is finite, despite the numerator and denominator of the original expression both vanishing.

For $q\gg1$, the exponential dominates the denominator:
$$
\boxed{j_0\sim D\beta^2F^2e^{-\beta F\delta}
=\frac{D}{\delta^2}q^2e^{-q}.}
$$
Opening a monomer-sized gap is exponentially rare at high opposing <force>. The algebraic $F^2$ prefactor belongs to this first-passage model and must not be discarded when quoting the leading asymptotic rate.

= f
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

Each addition starts a new statistically identical waiting episode at zero gap. Thus the addition rate is the reciprocal of a <mean first-passage time>, not the reciprocal of the mean gap:
$$
\boxed{\tau=\frac1{j_0}=\frac{e^{\beta F\delta}-\beta F\delta-1}{D\beta^2F^2}.}
$$
This can also be derived without the steady density. If $T(x)$ is the mean time to reach $\delta$ starting at gap $x$, its backward <Smoluchowski equation> is
$$
DT''-D\beta FT'=-1,\qquad T'(0)=0,\qquad T(\delta)=0.
$$
The first boundary is reflecting and the second absorbing. An <integrating factor> gives $T'(x)=[1-e^{\beta Fx}]/(D\beta F)$, whence
$$
T(0)=-\int_0^\delta T'(x)\,dx
=\frac{e^{\beta F\delta}-1-\beta F\delta}{D\beta^2F^2}=\tau.
$$
At weak <force>, $\tau\simeq\delta^2/(2D)$ is the time for a freely diffusing wall to move a <monomer> length, with reflection at the tip. At strong <force>, opening the gap requires <work> $F\delta$, with <Boltzmann distribution> suppression $e^{-\beta F\delta}$. The drift establishes a near-tip thermal length $\ell_F=k_BT/F$ and a <diffusion> time $\ell_F^2/D$; consequently
$$
\tau\sim\frac{\ell_F^2}{D}e^{\delta/\ell_F}.
$$
The exponentially long wait explains why the growth slows. There is no additional chemical waiting time in the imposed absorbing-threshold model; such a time would be needed to describe the separate <reaction-limited polymerization Brownian ratchet> regime.

= 6
{parent=Section B}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In a <magnesium chloride> <chemical solution>, the principal pair interactions are <hard sphere> exclusion, repulsion of overlapping <electric double layers>, and attractive <van der Waals forces>. The positive surface attracts <chloride> <counterions> and repels <magnesium> <coions>; mobile <ions> screen the bare <Coulomb's law> interaction. The competition between screened electrostatic repulsion and <van der Waals force> attraction is described by <DLVO theory>.

For well-separated identical weakly charged spheres, the approximate repulsive pair <potential energy>, at centre separation $R\geq2a$, is
$$
U_{\mathrm{el}}(R)\simeq\frac{Q^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0\varepsilon_r}
\frac{e^{-\kappa(R-2a)}}{(1+\kappa a)^2R}.
$$
It is a <screened Coulomb pair potential>. An attractive <Hamaker constant> adds a negative short-range contribution, while overlap is forbidden. Very near contact, <solvation> and molecular surface structure can also matter. No polymer-induced interaction should be assumed before polymer is added in part (f).

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The particle carries positive <surface charge density> $\sigma=Q/(4\pi a^2)$. Its surrounding <electric double layer> has an excess of negative <chloride> <counterions> and a deficit of positive <magnesium> <coions>. Thus $\phi(r)>0$ decreases to zero, $n_{\mathrm{Cl}}(r)$ decreases towards its bulk value, and $n_{\mathrm{Mg}}(r)$ increases towards its bulk value. The diffuse <charge density> is negative and tends to zero.

If $c$ denotes the bulk number of dissolved salt formula units per unit volume, the far-field <number densities> are $n_{\mathrm{Mg},\infty}=c$ and $n_{\mathrm{Cl},\infty}=2c$. The bulk is neutral even though these number densities differ. In the dilute mean-field picture the total diffuse <electric charge> is $-Q$, ensuring overall <electroneutrality>. A molecularly bound compact layer would require additional surface-chemistry information and is not assumed in the supplied potential.

\Image[2022-q6-ion-cloud.png]
{title=<Counterion> enrichment and <coion> depletion around a positive sphere}
{description=Original schematic. The left panel distinguishes the positive surface from an excess of chloride in the surrounding diffuse cloud; symbol positions are illustrative. The right panel plots the mean-field concentrations normalized separately to their bulk values, for a small illustrative positive surface potential. Both normalized curves tend to one, although bulk chloride number density is twice bulk magnesium number density.}

= c
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $\varepsilon=\varepsilon_0\varepsilon_r$ be the <permittivity>, $\beta=(k_BT)^{-1}$, $e>0$ the <elementary charge>, and $c$ the bulk salt formula-unit <number density>. The <Boltzmann distribution> gives
$$
n_{\mathrm{Mg}}(r)=c\,e^{-2\beta e\phi(r)},\qquad
n_{\mathrm{Cl}}(r)=2c\,e^{\beta e\phi(r)}.
$$
Consequently the exterior <Poisson-Boltzmann equation> is
$$
\frac1{r^2}\frac d{dr}\left(r^2\frac{d\phi}{dr}\right)
=-\frac{2ec}{\varepsilon}\left(e^{-2\beta e\phi}-e^{\beta e\phi}\right),\qquad r>a.
$$
For small $Q$, meaning $2\beta e|\phi(a)|\ll1$, linearize the exponentials. The <Debye–Hückel approximation> gives
$$
\rho_{\mathrm{ion}}\simeq-6\beta e^2c\phi,\qquad
\frac1{r^2}(r^2\phi')'=\kappa^2\phi,\qquad
\boxed{\kappa^2=\frac{6e^2c}{\varepsilon k_BT},\quad \lambda_D=\kappa^{-1}.}
$$
This is also $\kappa^2=\beta e^2\sum_i z_i^2 n_{i,\infty}/\varepsilon$: the divalent cation contributes $4c$ and the two monovalent anions contribute $2c$. In terms of molar salt concentration $C$ in $\mathrm{mol\,m^{-3}}$, replace $c$ by $N_A C$; if $C$ is in $\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, use $c=10^3N_A C$. The molar <ionic strength> is $3C$ in the same concentration units.

The boundary conditions are $\phi(\infty)=0$ and, by <Gauss's law>,
$$
-\varepsilon\phi'(a^+)=\frac{Q}{4\pi a^2}.
$$
Here the surface charge is uniform and the spherically symmetric interior field is zero. Set $u=r\phi$; the radial equation reduces to $u''=\kappa^2u$. Decay excludes the growing exponential, so write $\phi=B e^{-\kappa(r-a)}/r$. Then $\phi'(a)=-B(1+\kappa a)/a^2$, giving
$$
\boxed{\phi(r)=\frac{Q}{4\pi\varepsilon\,r(1+\kappa a)}e^{-\kappa(r-a)}.}
$$
It solves the *linearized* <Poisson-Boltzmann equation>, not the full nonlinear equation at finite $Q$. The radial ion profiles, consistently to first order, are
$$
\boxed{n_{\mathrm{Mg}}(r)\simeq c[1-2\beta e\phi(r)],\qquad
n_{\mathrm{Cl}}(r)\simeq2c[1+\beta e\phi(r)].}
$$
The exponential <Boltzmann distribution> formulas above express the full mean-field relation, but substituting a linearized potential into them is accurate only to the retained order. A useful <electroneutrality> check is
$$
4\pi\int_a^\infty\rho_{\mathrm{ion}}r^2dr
=-4\pi\varepsilon[r^2\phi']_a^\infty=-Q.
$$
The result is the <Debye–Hückel potential of a charged sphere>.

= d
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Write the particle <volume fraction> as $\eta=(4\pi a^3/3)n_p$. In a stable, repulsion-dominated <colloid>, low $\eta$ gives a disordered fluid. Increasing $\eta$ makes the interaction between neighboring particles important and can produce an ordered <crystal>, separated from the fluid by a <phase coexistence curve>. Increasing the <Debye–Hückel screening length> at fixed charge strengthens the repulsion at a given separation and allows freezing at smaller geometrical <volume fraction>.

The geometric intuition is that particles behave as though they occupy more than their bare diameter: roughly, an effective exclusion diameter $d_{\mathrm{eff}}$ extends out to the separation where $U_{\mathrm{el}}$ is of order $k_BT$. A <hard sphere> comparison then uses $\eta_{\mathrm{eff}}\sim\eta[d_{\mathrm{eff}}/(2a)]^3$. This explains the trend; it is not an exact freezing criterion.

At short <Debye–Hückel screening length> and negligible attraction, the diagram approaches <hard sphere> fluid–crystal coexistence. For longer-ranged soft repulsion, <body-centered cubic lattice> and <face-centered cubic lattice> crystals can both be stable, with the former often appearing towards the softer, lower-density freezing boundary. Screened-Coulomb simulations exhibit both these crystal structures.https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.57.2694[Kremer, Robbins and Grest, Phase Diagram of Yukawa Systems: Model for Charge-Stabilized Colloids]{ref}

\Image[2022-q6-phase-diagram.png]
{title=Schematic <phase diagram> for a stable repulsive <colloid>}
{description=Original qualitative fluid–crystal diagram at fixed radius, <temperature> and charge. The two curves bound a fluid–crystal coexistence band. Increased screening length allows freezing at smaller bare particle volume fraction. Crystal-structure boundaries and numerical coexistence positions are deliberately not assigned. The dashed arrow shows adding salt at fixed volume fraction; the second panel shows the direction of the boundary shift when surface charge increases.}

No universal quantitative diagram follows from $(\eta,\lambda_D)$ alone: the charge, <Hamaker constant> and surface conditions must also be fixed. If salt suppresses the stabilizing barrier enough for <van der Waals force> attraction to dominate, a colloid-poor/colloid-rich separation or aggregation can replace part of this repulsive reference diagram. Dense <colloidal glasses> or <colloidal gels> may also be observed after kinetic arrest; they should not be mistaken for additional <thermodynamic equilibrium> <thermodynamic phases>.

= e
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

First hold the surface <electric charge> fixed and increase the bulk salt concentration. Since $\kappa^2\propto c$, the <Debye–Hückel screening length> decreases as $c^{-1/2}$. For surface gap $h=R-2a\geq0$,
$$
U_{\mathrm{el}}(R)\propto\frac{e^{-\kappa h}}{(1+\kappa a)^2},
\qquad
\frac{\partial\log U_{\mathrm{el}}}{\partial\kappa}
=-h-\frac{2a}{1+\kappa a}<0.
$$
Thus both the range and the strength of repulsion decrease. In the repulsive <phase diagram>, the sample moves downwards at fixed <volume fraction> and can melt from a <crystal> into a fluid. Because screening length is already a diagram axis, changing salt moves the state through that diagram rather than independently changing its fixed-charge coexistence curves. If <van der Waals forces> become dominant, however, adding salt may instead destabilize the dispersion and cause aggregation. These are distinct physical regimes.

Second increase $\sigma$ at fixed salt concentration and particle size. The <electric charge> is $Q=4\pi a^2\sigma$, so the pair repulsion increases approximately as $\sigma^2$ within the <Debye–Hückel approximation>, while the reservoir screening length remains unchanged. Freezing then becomes possible at lower <volume fraction> or shorter screening length. At fixed $\lambda_D$, the coexistence curves shift towards lower $\eta$; a previously fluid sample may crystallize.

These statements assume fixed, uniform charge and a salt reservoir dominating the screening. Strong charging requires nonlinear screening and possibly charge regulation. At very low added salt or appreciable particle concentration, particle-released <counterions> also contribute to <ionic strength>, so the screening length is no longer independently fixed by the added salt alone.

= f
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

For dissolved, *nonadsorbing* <polyethylene glycol>, the usual new interaction is a <depletion force>. Polymer centres are excluded from a layer of thickness of order their <radius of gyration> $R_g$ around each particle. When the particles approach, their exclusion layers overlap. This increases the volume accessible to polymer and its translational <entropy>, lowering the colloid pair <free energy>. Equivalently the surrounding polymer <osmotic pressure> pushes the particles together.

In the dilute-polymer <Asakura–Oosawa model>, let $h=R-2a$ be the surface gap and $R_e=a+R_g$. Then
$$
\boxed{U_{\mathrm{dep}}(R)=-\Pi_p V_{\mathrm{ov}}(R),\qquad
V_{\mathrm{ov}}(R)=\frac\pi{12}(4R_e+R)(2R_e-R)^2}
$$
for $2a\leq R<2R_e$, and $U_{\mathrm{dep}}=0$ for $R\geq2R_e$. Here $\Pi_p\simeq n_p^{(\mathrm{polymer})}k_BT$ is the reservoir polymer <osmotic pressure>. The overlap-volume formula follows by adding two equal <spherical cap> volumes with height $R_e-R/2$. Thus the added attraction has range about $2R_g$ and grows with polymer concentration. The total interaction is approximately hard exclusion plus screened electrostatic repulsion plus <van der Waals force> and depletion attractions.

Increasing polymer concentration therefore creates a family of <phase diagrams>, rather than a unique shift determined by polymer size alone. Where the attraction overcomes repulsion, colloid-poor fluid can coexist with a colloid-rich phase, and fluid–crystal coexistence generally broadens. For sufficiently long-ranged attraction, stable fluid–fluid separation can occur. For the stipulated small polymer, the attraction is short-ranged: fluid–fluid separation can be metastable to crystallization, and a rapid attraction increase can instead produce an arrested <colloidal gel>. Residual long-ranged repulsion can oppose bulk separation. Simulations and free-volume calculations confirm that longer-ranged electrostatic repulsion can counteract depletion-driven separation.https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/0509790[Fortini, Dijkstra and Tuinier, Phase behaviour of charged colloidal sphere dispersions with added polymer chains]{ref}

The nonadsorption assumption matters. If the <polyethylene glycol> adsorbs to the particles, a dense attached layer can give <steric stabilization>, whereas chains attaching to two particles can produce <polymer bridging>. The paper does not specify surface affinity or polymer concentration, so depletion is the standard conditional answer, not a prediction of an inevitable particular final phase.
